<p>AI start-up Anthropic has filed a federal lawsuit in California to overturn the US Defense Department’s designation of the company as a “supply-chain risk to national security,” and to stop the government from enforcing a related blacklist across federal agencies. Anthropic says the designation is unlawful and violates its rights, arguing it was penalized for keeping limits on how its Claude AI models can be used by the military—especially limits meant to prevent mass surveillance of Americans and fully autonomous weapons. The Trump administration and Pentagon leaders say those restrictions create operational and national-security risks by letting a private contractor constrain “lawful military purposes,” and they have ordered agencies to stop using Anthropic’s technology. The case puts a broader question on trial—one felt well beyond the US—about how governments can adopt AI for defense while still keeping meaningful guardrails.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/u5mITYidwUtXsidlAMWOHy5iMejRfpXB6gGw8fQdA-sUZMgi2Sqckd2nVUZo4MlcnvGfKDWSqkW79_4BlATHLbP2n8zm5_ZEQ5U_GTBd_s2cwOz5nsX_AwSuwsnZk-VyVzXDfesGdolamhqDXwIIpXjgNpOBs1ciBFsAGUu-k5avZegMLF0YRM6nV0TtMWzqwxDfhVotxER8k4W13YVAGWLmIK7VvPooimxiAt-4d5bdV4h6fmkvLeVfF2nfwBJ5x5BK1mTE_3ewcv9d4AoFJZnzn4o9_28IDOIjbjcTa-4hqeCP5JaSFkL4-zPUoCXdkQjZf1xEgy1D_XBjlfqajFMbFGS6FfsEuEsK9CaBFnThGbxkie5TDyVn0mr9kgsLUA6XofDqTPJ2fM_Itt6EBUeGTW3NUioErkIY8VJTfZ7QmNjbMGDjrpnbm__Wb0oMcvzCnMnyiK4KffVdf72Urq_5G2xLScRbKC9ZI-zSlrCdGoiV' alt='A view related to the Pentagon-Anthropic legal dispute over the company’s designation as a national security supply-chain risk.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Blacklisting scope: Anthropic says the designation pressures Pentagon contractors and suppliers to steer clear of commercial work involving its models, because vendors must certify they do not use Anthropic systems on Defense Department-related work.</li><li>Company-first claim: In its complaint, Anthropic describes itself as a safety-focused lab founded to maximize positive outcomes for humanity, and says the government retaliated against that stance.</li><li>Despite the feud: Semafor reports that Claude still played a central role in the US military campaign against Iran, even as the dispute with the Pentagon intensified.</li><li>Unusual designation: Reports describe the “supply-chain risk” label as one typically aimed at firms tied to foreign adversaries, and Dawn reports Anthropic says it is the first US company ever publicly hit with the designation.</li><li>Rights claims: Anthropic says the designation violates its free speech and due process rights, and that the government lacked legal authority to impose it.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Anthropic: The company says the blacklist and “supply-chain risk” label are unlawful and retaliatory, and that they violate its free speech and due process rights. It wants a court order that reverses the designation and blocks agencies from enforcing it. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3346019/anthropic-sues-us-government-row-over-ai-use-military-deepens'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>US Defense Department: As described in reports, the Pentagon argues Anthropic’s limits on “lawful military purposes” create operational risk by giving a private contractor leverage over battlefield decision-making. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/news/634321-anthropic-sues-pentagon-killer-ai/'>RT</a>)</li><li>Legal observers (as cited): Semafor reports that some legal experts think Anthropic has a strong case because the relevant security laws target sabotage and spying—neither of which the company is accused of. (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/03/09/2026/anthropic-sues-the-pentagon-over-supply-chain-risk-designation'>Semafor</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3346019/anthropic-sues-us-government-row-over-ai-use-military-deepens'>Anthropic sues Tru